<details class="debrief">
  <summary>What this exercise is showing</summary>

  <p>Nothing in the first version is broken in a way a mouse would notice. Every control responds to a click. The problem is what the controls <em>are</em>: a <code>div</code> with a click handler is a rectangle that happens to react. It is not in the tab order, it does not respond to Enter or the space bar, and a screen reader announces it as nothing at all.</p>

  <p>There is a second failure layered underneath. The one control you <em>can</em> reach, the booking button, has its focus outline removed, so even a keyboard user who gets there cannot see where they are.</p>

  <p>This is not a small audience. Keyboard-only use covers people with motor conditions such as Parkinson's, tremor or RSI, people using switch access, eye tracking or voice control, screen reader users, and anybody whose trackpad has died. It also covers the developer in your team who navigates by keyboard because it is faster.</p>

  <h3>Questions for the group</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>How far did you get before you reached for the mouse? Be honest.</li>
    <li>Where in our product do we use a <code>div</code> or a styled <code>span</code> as a button, a tab, a card or a menu?</li>
    <li>Has anyone here removed a focus outline because it looked untidy? What did we replace it with?</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What good looks like</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Use the native element. A <code>button</code> is focusable, operable by Enter and space, and announced as a button, for free.</li>
    <li>If you must build a custom control, it needs a role, a tab stop, keyboard handlers and a focus state &mdash; all four, every time.</li>
    <li>Never remove a focus indicator without designing a better one. WCAG 2.2 also adds <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-not-obscured-minimum.html">2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured</a>, so sticky headers and cookie banners must not cover the focused control either.</li>
    <li>Tab through every new screen before it ships. It takes a minute and it catches most of this.</li>
  </ul>
</details>
